---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const cellCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '1.1 - 0.34*log(x)', label: 'cathode (reduction) potential', color: '#1f6f54', width: 2 },
    { expr: '-0.76 + 0.34*log(x)', label: 'anode (oxidation) potential', color: '#d9480f', width: 2 },
    { expr: '1.1 - 0.34*log(x) - (-0.76 + 0.34*log(x))', label: 'cell voltage = difference', color: '#1d4ed8', width: 2.4 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0.001, max: 10, label: 'ion concentration (mol/L), log scale' },
  y: { min: -1.2, max: 1.4, label: 'potential (V)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};

const redoxHalfOrder = {
  "prompt": "Zinc displaces copper from solution. Rebuild the bookkeeping, electrons included:",
  "items": [
    "Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ — oxidation: the metal surrenders",
    "Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu — reduction: the ion grabs them",
    "Add the halves — the electrons cancel",
    "Overall: Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu — the displacement as one line"
  ],
  "why": "Electrons lost must equal electrons gained — cancel them and the displacement stands alone."
};

const redoxTableMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each entry with its consequence:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "fluorine (+2.87 V)",
      "right": "tops the scale — rips electrons from almost anything"
    },
    {
      "left": "lithium (−3.04 V)",
      "right": "bottoms it — surrenders electrons eagerly, hence the best batteries"
    },
    {
      "left": "zinc (−0.76 V) + copper (+0.34 V)",
      "right": "the Daniell cell — about 1.10 V fresh"
    },
    {
      "left": "magnesium (−2.37 V) vs copper",
      "right": "Mg is the more eager anode — the pair delivers about 2.7 V"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Positive cell voltage means the reaction runs itself — the table is the equilibrium chapter's K, translated into volts."
};

const redoxBridgeSpot = {
  "prompt": "A summary of the Daniell cell contains one faulty line — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Current in the wire only — the moving charge never enters the liquid.",
    "Ions carry it through the electrolyte instead.",
    "Cations drift toward the cathode compartment; anions toward the anode.",
    "The salt bridge ferries the electrons across to complete the loop.",
    "Without it, the beakers polarise and the flow dies almost immediately."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "The bridge ferries IONS, not electrons — it carries the ionic current and cancels the build-up; electrons do all their travelling in the metal wire.",
  "why": "If electrons could swim, there would be no need for a bridge at all — and the halves could not be separated."
};

const redoxCryoPlace = {
  "prompt": "Cryolite's whole job is cutting the price of liquefying bauxite's oxide. Slide the marker to the operating temperature that buys.",
  "labels": [
    "0 °C",
    "500 °C",
    "1,000 °C",
    "1,500 °C",
    "2,000 °C"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 2200
  },
  "answer": 950,
  "tolerance": 60,
  "why": "About 950 °C, down from 2,072 °C — and the process still draws roughly 14,000 kWh per tonne, which is why smelters sit beside hydroelectric dams."
};

const redoxCellCompare = {
  "prompt": "Same redox, two jackets — drag the divider across the drawer:",
  "before": {
    "label": "primary cell",
    "text": "Runs one way and retires — alkaline AAs: forcing current back generates gas and heat rather than restored reactants."
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "secondary cell",
    "text": "Built reversible — lithium-ion shuttles Li⁺ between graphite layers and a metal-oxide cathode, surviving thousands of cycles since 1991."
  },
  "why": "One chemistry survives the shuttle, the other ruptures — the label on the pack encodes the electrode chemistry, not a charging trick."
};

const redoxBatJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "Batteries store electricity.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "They store chemical energy whose controlled release is electricity — the mass barely changes while it powers a city block."
    },
    {
      "s": "Any battery can be recharged if you push current in long enough.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Primary cells' reactions are not reversible by design — forcing current back makes gas and heat instead of restored reactants."
    },
    {
      "s": "Anode = oxidation and cathode = reduction, always — which one is positive depends on the mode.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Discharging: cathode positive. Electrolysing: anode positive. Learn the chemistry, not the polarity."
    },
    {
      "s": "A 'dead' battery is simply one whose redox has run to equilibrium.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "No difference in pulling power left — the gap between the half-cell potentials has closed."
    }
  ]
};

const redoxRustEst = {
  "prompt": "Rusting eats infrastructure nobody ordered. Roughly what share of national output do wealthy countries lose to it each year?",
  "unit": "% of GDP",
  "answer": 3.5,
  "factor": 1.5,
  "why": "An estimated 3–4% of GDP — the largest unintended electrochemistry programme on Earth.",
  "context": "Salt accelerates the damage by making the electrolyte conduct better — coastal and de-iced roads rust hardest."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/intermediate/redox-and-electrochemistry">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    You need three foundational lessons behind you: Atomic Structure (electrons, ions,
    shells), the Periodic Table (which atoms hold electrons tightly), and Mole and
    Stoichiometry (balanced equations). Chemical Equilibrium and Thermodynamics and
    Enthalpy are helpful companions, because this chapter translates their ideas into
    volts — but the electron bookkeeping itself starts from zero.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>assign oxidation states and balance half-equations, electrons included;</li>
    <li>describe an electrochemical cell part by part, and say exactly what the salt bridge is for;</li>
    <li>use standard electrode potentials to predict cell voltages and which metal displaces which;</li>
    <li>predict electrolysis products for molten and aqueous cases, and explain why aluminium costs so much energy;</li>
    <li>compare batteries, fuel cells and corrosion as one redox chemistry in different jackets.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Redox</strong> reactions transfer electrons: one substance is oxidised (loses
    electrons), another is reduced (gains them — remember OIL RIG). Run the two halves in
    separate beakers, connect them with a wire and a salt bridge, and the forced electron journey
    becomes an <strong>electric current</strong> — the battery, invented by Volta in 1800,
    reimagined by every phone you own. Reverse the push and you drive non-spontaneous
    reactions instead: <strong>electrolysis</strong>, the industrial source of aluminium,
    chlorine and hydrogen. This chapter is electron bookkeeping from first principles:
    first the accounting rules, then the cells that make electrons flow usefully, then the
    cells (batteries, fuel cells, rusting ships) where the same chemistry runs unsupervised.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a cell's voltage is the difference between its two half-reactions — concentration shifts both, and the cell drains" widget="plotter" config={cellCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> at the right-hand end (high concentration) the blue
    cell voltage sits at about 1.1 V — the fresh Daniell cell. Slide the concentration
    down (the axis is logarithmic) and watch the two half-cell potentials drag toward each
    other while the blue difference narrows: a discharging battery is this graph moving
    left. The curves never meet while reactants remain — no difference in pulling power,
    no voltage.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> redox reactions transfer electrons, run forward as batteries
    and backwards as electrolysis. The chapter starts with the accounting rules that make
    the transfer trackable.
  </p>

  <p>
    The blue curve is what a voltmeter reads between a copper cathode (+0.34 V standard) and a
    zinc anode (−0.76 V): about 1.1 V fresh. As the battery discharges, concentrations drift and
    the Nernst equation drags the curves toward each other — dead battery = no difference in
    pulling power. Rechargeable cells simply reverse the current to un-drain them.
  </p>

  <h2>Oxidation states: the accountant's toolkit</h2>
  <p>
    Despite the name, oxidation has nothing essential to do with oxygen: <strong>oxidation is
    electron loss, reduction is electron gain</strong>, however the atoms rearrange. To track
    the transfer, assign <strong>oxidation states</strong> — charges atoms would carry if every
    compound were ionic: free elements 0; oxygen −2 (except peroxides); hydrogen +1 with
    non-metals; and the sum equal to the overall charge. Worked examples: in Fe₂O₃, oxygen
    takes −6 total, so iron averages +3 — rusting is iron 0 → +3 (oxidation) while oxygen 0 →
    −2 (reduction). In CH₄, carbon is −4; in CO₂ it is +4 — photosynthesis runs carbon +4 →
    −4, an eight-electron climb powered by light, and respiration is the descent: biology's
    biggest redox pair. The oxidising agent is the species that <em>gains</em> electrons (it
    oxidises the other party while being reduced itself); the reducing agent loses them. One
    discipline makes the whole system work: electrons are neither created nor destroyed, so
    oxidation and reduction <strong>always come in matched pairs</strong> — find one, the
    other is hiding nearby.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Half-equations</strong> make the pairing explicit. Zinc displacing copper from
    solution splits into Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ (oxidation) and Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu (reduction); add
    them and the electrons cancel, giving the overall Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu. More elaborate
    cases (manganate(VII) titrations: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O) balance charge as
    well as atoms — a skill the exam drills and laboratory redox titrations run on.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="put the electron bookkeeping in order" widget="order" config={redoxHalfOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> oxidation states and half-equations make electron transfer
    explicit and auditable — electrons lost must equal electrons gained. Now separate the
    two halves into different beakers and make the electrons do useful work on the way.
  </p>

  <h2>The electrochemical cell: chemistry as voltage</h2>
  <p>
    Separate the two half-reactions into two beakers — zinc strip in zinc sulfate, copper
    strip in copper sulfate — connect the metals with a wire and the solutions with a
    <strong>salt bridge</strong> (an ion pathway that keeps both beakers neutral, without
    which charge builds up and current stops within seconds). Electrons now flow through the
    wire <em>only</em>, doing work: this is the Daniell cell, and its voltage is the
    difference in electron-pulling power between the two half-cells. That pulling power is
    tabulated as <strong>standard electrode potentials</strong>, measured against the
    hydrogen half-cell (0 V by definition). The scale ranks oxidising greed: fluorine tops
    it (+2.87 V, rips electrons from almost anything), lithium bottoms it (−3.04 V,
    surrenders electrons eagerly — precisely why lithium sits at the heart of the best
    batteries). Any cell voltage is cathode minus anode: Zn/Cu gives +0.34 − (−0.76) =
    <strong>1.10 V</strong>. To pair magnesium with copper instead, the table decides the
    roles: Mg (−2.37 V) is the more eager anode, so Mg/Cu delivers about 2.7 V. Positive cell
    voltage means the reaction is spontaneous — the table is the equilibrium chapter's K,
    translated into volts.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the faulty line — what actually crosses the liquid" widget="spot-error" config={redoxBridgeSpot} />
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each entry on the potential table with what it buys" widget="match" config={redoxTableMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> electrons travel through the salt bridge (or through the solutions) to complete the circuit. They never enter the liquid: charge crosses the electrolyte as moving ions — cations drifting toward the cathode compartment, anions toward the anode — while the electrons do all their travelling in the metal wire. The salt bridge exists precisely to carry that ionic current and cancel the charge build-up; if electrons could swim, there would be no need for one, and the half-reactions could not be separated at all.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a cell is two half-reactions wired together, and its voltage
    is the difference in electron-pulling power, read off a table measured against
    hydrogen. That same table, wearing everyday clothes, predicts a whole catalogue of
    chemistry.
  </p>

  <h2>Displacement: the reactivity series in action</h2>
  <p>
    Drop a strip of zinc into copper sulfate solution and the strip grows copper-coloured
    while the blue fades: zinc, higher in the reactivity series, displaces copper — the
    electrons move from the more eager metal to the more greedy ion. The reactivity series
    (K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al, Zn, Fe, Pb, (H), Cu, Ag, Au) is really a ranked table of standard
    potentials in everyday clothing, and it predicts a surprising amount: which metals
    survive acids (anything above hydrogen fizzes), which can be extracted from their ores
    by carbon (below carbon: iron, from the blast furnace) and which demand electrolysis
    (above carbon: aluminium — the reason the light metal was a luxury until the
    Hall–Héroult process of 1886, so precious that, as the story goes, Napoleon III served
    his most honoured guests from aluminium cutlery while lesser diners got
    gold). Thermodynamics, metallurgy and dinner-party history in one ranked list.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the reactivity series ranks electron-eagerness, and everything
    from displacement reactions to the blast furnace follows from it. Push the other way —
    drive non-spontaneous chemistry with an outside current — and the series runs
    backwards.
  </p>

  <h2>Electrolysis: running the movie backwards</h2>
  <p>
    Push current <em>into</em> a cell and you force the non-spontaneous version: reduction at
    the negative cathode, oxidation at the positive anode (note the flip — in electrolysis
    the <em>anode is positive</em>, unlike a battery; the word means "where oxidation
    happens", and the sign depends on who is driving). <strong>Molten</strong> compounds give
    their elements simply: molten lead bromide deposits lead at the cathode and brown bromine
    at the anode. <strong>Aqueous</strong> solutions add a competitor — water itself, so you
    predict by reactivity: brine (concentrated NaCl) electrolyses to hydrogen at the cathode
    (water wins over sodium), chlorine at the anode (chloride wins over water at high
    concentration), leaving sodium hydroxide — the chlor-alkali process, source of
    much of the world's chlorine, caustic soda and bleach. The industrial flagship is
    <strong>aluminium</strong>: bauxite's Al₂O₃ dissolved in molten cryolite (to drop the
    melting point from 2,072 °C to about 950 °C) and electrolysed at enormous scale — the
    Hall–Héroult process runs on roughly 14,000 kWh per tonne, which is why aluminium smelters
    sit beside hydroelectric dams and why "recycled aluminium" (95% less energy) is one of
    civilisation's best bargains. Electroplating, copper refining (99.99% cathode copper) and
    the chlorine-water demonstration all use the same anatomy: power supply, electrodes,
    electrolyte, products at each pole.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="slide to the temperature cryolite buys" widget="place" config={redoxCryoPlace} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> electrolysis forces non-spontaneous redox by pushing current
    in — anode-positive flip included. Let the reaction run itself instead and you have
    the battery: the same chemistry, unsupervised, on your desk and in your pocket.
  </p>

  <h2>Batteries and fuel cells: electrons on demand</h2>
  <p>
    A battery is a redox reaction in a straitjacket — its electrons forced through your
    device instead of reacting locally. <strong>Primary cells</strong> (alkaline AAs) run
    one way and retire; <strong>secondary cells</strong> reverse the current to reset them.
    The <strong>lithium-ion</strong> cell, ubiquitous since 1991, shuttles Li⁺ between
    graphite layers (anode) and a metal-oxide cathode (usually cobalt/nickel oxides):
    discharge pulls lithium out of the graphite, charge pushes it back — "intercalation",
    chemistry you are reading this on. Lithium's appeal is the electrode-potential table
    plus arithmetic: the most negative standard potential, the lightest metal (6.9 g/mol),
    hence today's ~250 Wh/kg cells. Every battery carries the same physics as the widget:
    voltage falls as reactants drain and the Nernst equation closes the gap between the two
    half-cell potentials.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="drag the divider across the battery drawer" widget="compare" config={redoxCellCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> any battery can be recharged if you push current in long enough. The cell's chemistry decides: primary cells (alkaline AAs) run reactions that are not reversible by design — forcing current back generates gas and heat rather than restored reactants, which is how "recharging" becomes a rupture. Secondary cells are built reversible: their intercalation chemistry (lithium shuttling in and out of graphite) survives thousands of cycles. Rechargeability is a design property, not a voltage trick.</p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="true or false — batteries and their names" widget="judge" config={redoxBatJudge} />
  <p>
    A <strong>fuel cell</strong> avoids draining altogether by feeding the reactants in
    continuously. The hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell — with a proton-exchange membrane between
    the electrodes — runs anode: H₂ → 2H⁺ + 2e⁻, cathode: O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O, overall:
    2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Efficiency reaches around 60% (vs ~35% for the best combustion
    engines), the exhaust is water, and fuelled vehicles (buses, forklifts, some cars) refill
    in minutes. The catch is upstream: hydrogen is an energy <em>carrier</em>, not a source —
    green hydrogen needs electrolysis powered by renewables (the thermodynamics chapter's
    electricity bill), and storage demands high pressure or metal hydrides. Fuel cells turn
    the hydrogen economy from slogan into engineering specification.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrosion: electrochemistry nobody ordered</h2>
  <p>
    Rusting is a spontaneous redox eating the infrastructure: iron oxidises (Fe → Fe²⁺ →
    Fe³⁺) while oxygen and water reduce at neighbouring sites — a wet nail is millions of
    microscopic short-circuited cells, and salt accelerates it by making the "electrolyte"
    conduct better (which is why coastal and de-iced roads rust hardest). Both ingredients —
    oxygen <em>and</em> water — are required: iron kept in dry air or boiled, deoxygenated
    water barely rusts, a classic controlled-variable experiment. Protection is then applied
    electrochemistry: <strong>galvanising</strong> coats iron in zinc, which works even when
    scratched because zinc (more negative potential) corrodes <em>preferentially</em> —
    a <strong>sacrificial anode</strong>; the same trick guards ships' hulls and buried
    pipelines with bolted-on zinc or magnesium blocks. Paint isolates; sacrificial metals
    redirect; both are electrode-potential arithmetic with a paintbrush.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "batteries store electricity." They store
    <strong>chemical energy</strong> whose controlled release is electricity — a battery's
    mass barely changes while it powers a city block's worth of electronics, and a "dead"
    battery is simply one whose redox has run to equilibrium. Related confusion worth
    clearing: the electrodes' names. Anode = oxidation, cathode = reduction, always; which
    one is positive depends on whether the cell is discharging (cathode +) or being
    electrolysed (anode +). Learn the chemistry, not the polarity.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In the Zn/Cu cell, do electrons cross the salt bridge? Say what actually carries charge through the liquid.</li>
    <li>Why can an alkaline AA battery not be recharged, while a lithium-ion cell survives thousands of cycles?</li>
    <li>Iron rusts; galvanised iron does not, even when the coating is scratched. What does zinc do that paint cannot?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Never — electrons travel only through the metal wire.
    Charge crosses the electrolyte as moving ions: cations drift toward the cathode
    compartment, anions toward the anode, which is precisely the salt bridge's job.
    (2) A primary cell's reactions are not reversible by design — forcing current back
    generates gas and heat instead of restored reactants. Secondary cells are built
    reversible: lithium shuttles in and out of graphite without destroying the electrode.
    (3) Zinc sits lower in the potential table, so it corrodes preferentially — a
    sacrificial anode that keeps protecting the iron where the coat is broken. Paint only
    isolates, and its first scratch is its last.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Redox is electron transfer: oxidation is loss, reduction is gain, and they always come in matched pairs.</li>
    <li>Oxidation states and half-equations are the bookkeeping tools; the electrons must balance.</li>
    <li>A cell's voltage is the difference between its half-cell potentials, tabulated against the hydrogen half-cell.</li>
    <li>Electrons travel through the wire only; ions carry the current through the electrolyte and salt bridge.</li>
    <li>The reactivity series is electrode potentials in everyday clothing — it predicts displacement, extraction and corrosion.</li>
    <li>Electrolysis runs redox backwards; the anode is positive during electrolysis, negative during discharge.</li>
    <li>Batteries store chemical energy, not electricity; rechargeability is a design property of the chemistry.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Chemical Equilibrium is the formal companion to this chapter's voltage table — the
    same spontaneity, written as K — and Thermodynamics and Enthalpy prices the
    electrolysis bill properly. The same electron bookkeeping reappears later in biology
    as respiration and photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The Nernst equation: voltage as concentration gauge.</strong> Half-cell
    potentials shift with concentration by E = E° − (0.059/n)·log Q (at 25 °C) — the widget's
    drifting curves. Practical fruits: pH meters (the glass electrode is a concentration
    cell reading hydrogen-ion activity), blood-gas analysers, and the "battery test under
    load" that separates a charged cell from a charge-starved one. Tenfold concentration
    change shifts a one-electron potential by 59 mV — small numbers, precisely measured,
    enormous consequences.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The price of spontaneity — and its absence.</strong> Corrosion costs developed
    economies an estimated 3–4% of GDP annually — the largest unintended electrochemistry
    programme on Earth. Conversely, forcing non-spontaneous chemistry has its own bill:
    producing hydrogen by electrolysis costs more energy than the hydrogen returns when
    used (the first law is not negotiable), which is why "green hydrogen" is an economics
    and renewable-capacity question before it is a chemistry one. Electrochemistry never
    violates the energy ledger; it just chooses where the electrons cross.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="call the bill for rust nobody ordered" widget="estimate" config={redoxRustEst} />
  <p>
    <strong>Biological redox: the electron economy of life.</strong> Respiration is a
    controlled redox cascade — glucose's electrons pass through the electron transport
    chain, each step a small potential drop pumping protons, and ATP synthase cashes the
    gradient. Photosynthesis runs the reverse climb. Every protein in those chains is
    calibrated like a battery: NAD⁺/NADH at −0.32 V, O₂/H₂O at +0.82 V — the same table
    this chapter ranks metals with, now organising metabolism. Poisons that interrupt it
    (cyanide at the cytochrome end) are electrochemistry with a body count.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open frontiers.</strong> Solid-state batteries (replace the flammable liquid
    electrolyte with a ceramic), sodium-ion cells (cheap, abundant chemistry for grid
    storage), redox-flow batteries (pumping liquid electrolytes through a cell — tanks of
    stored electrons for renewables), and overpotential research (why electrolysis always
    costs more voltage than thermodynamics demands, and how catalysts shave it). Each is a
    live version of this chapter's core move: potential differences, harnessed. The
    discipline Volta started with stacked coins now negotiates the energy transition.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Volta (1800); Nernst (1889); OpenStax, Chemistry 2e (ch. 17, Electrochemistry); International Aluminium Institute energy data; US NACE corrosion cost study; DOE hydrogen and fuel cell program fact sheets. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
